## How the scripts work together

```
YOUR ROM ZIP
    │
    │  bash extract_payload.sh
    ▼
input/product.img ──────────────────────────── (stock, NEVER modified)
    │                                                    │
    │  sudo bash inject_apps.sh                          │ kept forever as clean baseline
    │  (reads APKs from product/ folder)                 │
    ▼
output/product.img ─── bash flash_product.sh ──► YOUR PHONE ✓
                                                (/product partition now has
                                                 your apps as true system apps)

extract_payload.sh also writes:
output/vbmeta.img  ─── flash once (Step 2) ──► disables Android Verified Boot
output/vbmeta_system.img  (if present in ROM)
```

**Key design:** `input/product.img` is your permanent stock baseline — it is never modified by any script. Every run of `inject_apps.sh` starts by copying it fresh to `output/product.img`, then injects your apps into the copy. This means:
- Re-running always produces a clean, predictable result
- Removing an app from `product/` actually removes it from the next output
- You never need to re-extract from the ROM just to start fresh

