## PART 1 — First-time setup for a new device

Do this entire Part once per device. When you just want to update an app later, skip to Part 2.

---

### Step 0 — Get your ROM file

Your ROM is distributed as a `.zip` file. Place it directly in the `payload_(or)_ROM-file/` folder:

```bash
cp ~/Downloads/your-rom-filename.zip /path/to/FLASHING/payload_(or)_ROM-file/
```

That's all — you don't need to open the zip or find `payload.bin` inside it. The extraction script handles everything.

---

### Step 1 — Extract partition images

```bash
cd /path/to/FLASHING/
bash extract_payload.sh
```

The script automatically:
- Finds your ROM zip (or `payload.bin`) in the `payload_(or)_ROM-file/` folder
- Installs `payload_dumper` if it isn't on your system yet
- Extracts `product.img` → `input/` (the stock baseline `inject_apps.sh` always starts from)
- Extracts `vbmeta.img` and `vbmeta_system.img` → `output/` (for the one-time AVB disable flash)
- Handles `vbmeta_system` being absent on some devices gracefully

You'll see a summary at the end showing what was extracted and the exact fastboot commands to run next.

> **What is payload.bin?** Modern Android A/B update packages store all partition data in a single binary called `payload.bin` inside the ROM zip. `payload_dumper` reads this format and extracts individual partition images directly from the zip — no need to unzip the ROM manually.

> **If you already have a raw `payload.bin`**, just place it in `payload_(or)_ROM-file/payload.bin` — the script detects it automatically.

---

### Step 2 — Disable dm-verity (the phone's partition security)

Android Verified Boot (AVB) cryptographically signs every partition so the phone can detect tampering. We need to disable this so the bootloader accepts our modified `product.img`.

> **Important:** This only works if your bootloader is unlocked. On a custom ROM it always is. On stock firmware it usually isn't.

**Boot your phone into fastboot mode:**
```bash
adb reboot bootloader
# Or hold Power + Volume Down while booting (varies by device)
```

**Verify the device is visible:**
```bash
fastboot devices
# Should print:  abae8b16    fastboot
```

**Disable verification on the vbmeta partitions:**
```bash
# Always: patch both slots of the main vbmeta
fastboot flash vbmeta_a   --disable-verity --disable-verification output/vbmeta.img
fastboot flash vbmeta_b   --disable-verity --disable-verification output/vbmeta.img

# Only if output/vbmeta_system.img exists:
fastboot flash vbmeta_system_a   --disable-verity --disable-verification output/vbmeta_system.img
fastboot flash vbmeta_system_b   --disable-verity --disable-verification output/vbmeta_system.img

# Reboot to confirm the phone still boots
fastboot reboot
```

> **Why `_a` and `_b`?** A/B devices have two copies of every partition so they can update one while booting from the other. You must patch both.

> **Why does `--disable-verity` come before the filename?** This is a fastboot quirk — the flags must precede the image path, otherwise they are silently ignored.

You only need to do this **once per device**. AVB stays disabled across reboots and ROM updates (as long as you keep the patched vbmeta).

---

### Step 3 — Add your apps to the `product/` folder

The `product/` folder is your APK source tree. Think of it like a blueprint — whatever is in here gets injected into the partition image.

**`app/` vs `priv-app/` — which one to use:**

| Use `app/` for | Use `priv-app/` for |
|----------------|---------------------|
| Any regular app | App stores (AuroraStore, F-Droid) |
| Media players, browsers, note apps | VPN apps |
| File managers, barcode scanners | Audio system tools (RootlessJamesDSP) |
| Anything you just want pre-installed | Anything that installs/uninstalls other apps silently |

When in doubt, use `app/`. The only real difference is that `priv-app/` apps can request special permissions that `app/` apps cannot.

**To add a single APK:**
```bash
mkdir product/app/NewPipe
cp ~/Downloads/NewPipe.apk product/app/NewPipe/NewPipe.apk
```

**To add a split APK bundle (`.apkm` from APKMirror):**
```bash
# 1. Rename .apkm to .zip and extract
cp ~/Downloads/app.apkm /tmp/app.zip
unzip /tmp/app.zip -d /tmp/app/

# 2. Create the folder and copy all the APKs you want
mkdir product/app/FirefoxFocus
cp /tmp/app/base.apk                    product/app/FirefoxFocus/
cp /tmp/app/split_config.arm64_v8a.apk  product/app/FirefoxFocus/
cp /tmp/app/split_config.xxhdpi.apk     product/app/FirefoxFocus/   # optional, tiny
```

The script automatically skips architecture splits that don't match your device (e.g., `x86_64` splits on an ARM phone), so you can safely copy all `.apk` files from the bundle and let the script filter them.

**To update an existing app:**
```bash
cp ~/Downloads/NewPipe-new-version.apk product/app/NewPipe/NewPipe.apk
```

**To remove an app from the system:**
```bash
rm -rf product/app/NewPipe
# Next inject run will not include it
```

---

### Step 4 — Inject the apps into the partition image

Connect your phone via USB (optional — if connected, the script auto-detects your CPU type; if not, it uses sensible defaults).

```bash
sudo bash inject_apps.sh
```

The script does all of this automatically:
- Detects your device's CPU architecture via ADB
- Copies `input/product.img` → `output/product.img` (fresh start)
- Calculates how much space is needed and **grows the partition if necessary**
- Injects all compatible APKs
- Pre-extracts native libraries (`.so` files) alongside each APK so they load correctly from the read-only partition
- Sets correct Linux permissions and SELinux labels

You'll see output like:
```
  ABI detected from connected device: arm64-v8a armeabi-v7a armeabi

==> Preparing output image...
  Copying 1.2G  input/product.img → output/product.img
  Done.

==> Checking space requirements...
  Net new data needed : 450 MB
  Free space in image : 580 MB
  Sufficient space — no resize needed

Mounted output/product.img → /mnt/product_edit

==> Injecting app/
  + app/NewPipe  (none)
  + app/FirefoxFocus  (arm64-v8a) [split: 3 APKs]
    extracted native libs → lib/
  SKIP (ABI x86_64 not in {arm64-v8a ...}): SomeX86OnlyApp

==> Injecting priv-app/
  + priv-app/AuroraStore  (none)

==> Done. Unmounted cleanly.
    Installed : 3 apps
    Skipped   : 1 apps (ABI-incompatible)
```

**What "SKIP (ABI)" means:** The APK contains native code compiled only for a CPU your phone doesn't have. An x86-only app would crash instantly on an ARM phone. Replace it with a universal or arm64 build.

**If the partition needs to grow** (your apps are larger than the stock free space), the script handles it automatically — you'll see an `Auto-growing image by X MB` message. No manual steps needed.

---

### Step 5 — Flash the image to your phone

```bash
bash flash_product.sh
```

That's it. The script handles everything:
- If your phone is in Android mode, it reboots it to fastboot automatically
- Switches to fastbootd (userspace fastboot) if your device uses dynamic partitions
- Detects whether your device has A/B slots and flashes both
- Reboots the phone when done

**First boot is slow — this is completely normal.** Android compiles ("dex-optimizes") every new system app during first boot. With many large apps this can take 2–5 minutes. The boot animation will loop. Do not panic.

