## Troubleshooting

**`inject_apps.sh`: "Input image not found: input/product.img"**
You haven't extracted `product.img` yet. Place your ROM zip in `payload_(or)_ROM-file/` and run:
```bash
bash extract_payload.sh
```

**"mount: /mnt/product_edit: can't read superblock"**
The image file is corrupt. Re-extract a fresh copy:
```bash
bash extract_payload.sh
```

**"inject_apps.sh: pre-scan mount failed — is the image already mounted?"**
A previous run crashed and left the image mounted. Unmount it:
```bash
sudo umount /mnt/product_edit
```

**"fastboot: Partition not found" when flashing manually**
You're in bootloader fastboot but your device uses dynamic partitions. Switch to fastbootd first:
```bash
fastboot reboot fastboot    # enters fastbootd (userspace fastboot)
fastboot flash product_a output/product.img
```
`flash_product.sh` handles this automatically — use the script instead of manual fastboot commands.

**Apps crash immediately on open**
Two likely causes:
1. **Missing native library** — the APK was a split bundle and you only copied `base.apk` without the architecture split (`split_config.arm64_v8a.apk`). Add the missing split to the app's folder and re-inject.
2. **ABI mismatch** — the APK has native code built only for x86. Get an arm64 or universal build.

**"FAILED (remote: 'This partition doesn't exist')" for vbmeta_system**
Your device doesn't have a `vbmeta_system` partition. Skip those two flash commands — only flash `vbmeta_a` and `vbmeta_b`.

**Phone bootloops after flashing**
The image is likely corrupted or ran out of space mid-injection. Re-extract a clean stock image and retry:
```bash
bash extract_payload.sh
# Remove some APKs from product/ if space was the issue, then:
sudo bash inject_apps.sh
bash flash_product.sh
```

**First boot takes forever (>10 minutes)**
Give it up to 15 minutes on slower phones with many large APKs. If it genuinely never finishes, force reboot (hold Power 10 seconds) and check if the system boots normally on the next attempt.

**Apps installed but device shows them as "not installed" or can't be opened**
If you previously had a user-installed version of the same app, there can be a signature conflict. Uninstall the user version first:
```bash
adb shell pm uninstall com.package.name.here
```

